analysisWe need to parse the scenario: This appears to be a case involving the United Macedonian Organisation Ilinden – PIRIN (UMO Ilinden – PIRIN), a political organization representing Macedonian ethnicity, trying to organize rallies in Sofia, Bulgaria, in August/September 1998. The mayor of Sofia prohibited the rallies using the Meetings and Marches Act (MMA). The applicants appealed but faced procedural obstacles: they appealed to the Sofia District Court, which said they must first appeal to the Executive Committee of the People's Council per section 12(4) of MMA. However, the Executive Committee no longer existed after constitutional changes (1991 constitution). The Municipal Council was considered the successor but didn't have explicit authority. The district court sent them to the Executive Committee anyway. They then appealed to the Sofia City Court, which upheld the requirement to appeal to the Municipal Council, etc., and eventually the Supreme Court of Cassation dismissed the appeal, stating lack of interest.

Thus, the applicants were unable to hold the rally due to the mayor's ban, which remained in effect. The question is whether there was a violation of Article 11.

We need to go through each canonical element:

(a) Scope: i) Has there been an assembly? ii) Did the applicant try to participate? iii) Was the assembly peaceful?

We need to see if the rally qualifies as an assembly. According to the facts, the applicants intended to hold a rally (public gathering) in front of the National Theatre in Sofia. That is an assembly (meeting/rally). The applicants attempted to organize it, and presumably would have participated. The rally was promised to be peaceful (the applicants assured the mayor that it would be peaceful). So yes, scope satisfied.

